## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to translate edge ML costs into a robust business case, including privacy constraints, rollout risk, and financial metrics.

## Key Concepts
- On-device RL inference energy and latency
- Policy delta encoding and secure transmission
- Drift/maintenance costs and OTA rollout
- Privacy/compliance and data minimization
- Rollout strategy and go/no-go criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-cost estimator
function estimateCost(params) {
  const {
    dispatchesPerHour, energyPerDispatch, latencyMs,
    cloudSavingsPerDispatch
  } = params;
  // simplified calculation
  return energyPerDispatch * dispatchesPerHour / 3600;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate model drift in production and trigger rollback?
- What would you put in the go/no-go criteria to scale beyond pilot?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design privacy-preserving, offline-first collaborative systems at scale. It tests technical understanding of cryptographic protocols, peer-to-peer networking, CRDT algorithms, and cost-benefit analysis while considering regulatory compliance and user experience constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Privacy-preserving collaborative editing
- CRDT convergence and conflict-free resolution
- Peer-to-peer mesh topology and infrastructure costs
- End-to-end encryption with lifecycle key management
- Compliance logging and audit trail requirements
- Performance trade-offs between latency and device resource consumption

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple OR-Set CRDT merge (illustrative)
function mergeORSet(A, B) {
  const merged = new Set([...A.added, ...B.added]);
  [...A.removed, ...B.removed].forEach(item => merged.delete(item));
  return merged;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates practical cost-benefit analysis thinking for privacy-conscious mobile features with offline-first architecture. It tests understanding of storage trade-offs, energy budgeting, consent management, and long-term maintenance considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first storage and local geofence processing to ensure reliability without network connectivity
- Energy budgeting for continuous location monitoring and CPU usage for geofence checks
- Privacy, consent, and data minimization requirements for location-based features
- Cloud synchronization trade-offs for shared calendar data and conflict resolution
- Rollout planning with phased deployment and offline fallback behavior
- Technical debt and drift/maintenance costs over the feature lifecycle

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: register geofence, trigger locally, and schedule reminder
